== (i) greybody factor: record scattering vs the closed form ==
  profile N^2 = 1/(1+e^(-2 kappa y)), m_perp = 1.0, kappa = 0.5  (T_H = kappa/2pi = 0.07958)
   omega     R_record        R_exact         |diff|
    1.05   0.01753891    0.01753891    1.5e-14
    1.20   0.00022365    0.00022365    5.4e-16
    1.50   0.00000065    0.00000065    4.7e-17
    2.00   0.00000000    0.00000000    4.2e-19
  -> the record mode reflects off the lapse-squared barrier at
     exactly the closed-form greybody factor.

== (ii) sharp-step limit = P12's impedance law; adiabatic limit ==
   kappa =   2.0:  R_exact = 0.006275
   kappa =   8.0:  R_exact = 0.019543
   kappa =  32.0:  R_exact = 0.021172
   sharp-interface impedance law ((k-q)/(k+q))^2 = 0.021286
   kappa = 0.125: R_exact = 3.92e-25  (adiabatic: transparent)
  -> the greybody filter interpolates the P12 interface physics:
     it is the impedance mismatch smoothed over the thermal record
     width 1/(4 pi T_H); hot horizons scatter like sharp seams,
     cold ones are transparent.

== (iii) record lattice packet vs R(omega_0) ==
  packet omega_0 = 1.2, bandwidth 1/sigma = 0.083:
  reflected energy fraction = 0.05079
  bandwidth-averaged R      = 0.05080   (monochromatic R = 0.04507)
  |diff| = 1.7e-05
  -> the record dynamics itself realizes the greybody filter.

== (iv) below the barrier: total reflection ==
   omega = 0.5: |R - 1| = 2.2e-16   (evanescent transmitted channel: unitarity forces R = 1)
   omega = 0.9: |R - 1| = 0.0e+00   (evanescent transmitted channel: unitarity forces R = 1)
  -> massive record modes below the barrier top cannot escape to
     the flat region: the horizon's emission in each massive
     channel opens only above m_perp - the greybody gap.
== p13d done ==
